The catch block in the `extractPropsFromStories` loop swallows all errors silently. When extraction fails for a component (type checker error, unresolved import, assertion failure, etc.), the `component.reactComponentMeta` simply stays `undefined`, making it look identical to the case where the component was skipped intentionally.

Add at minimum a `logger.debug(...)` call, e.g.:
```typescript
} catch (err) {
  logger.debug(`[reactComponentMeta] Failed to extract props for ${entryComponent?.componentName}: ${err}`);
  continue;
}
```
Without this, debugging extraction failures in production is nearly impossible.

`ensureFiles` performs an O(n) `Array.includes()` search for every `fileName` in the input. For a project with N tracked files, adding M new files is O(N×M). `extractPropsFromStories` calls `ensureFiles` on every batch invocation (including background warmup ticks), so for large design systems (Mantine at 9 tsconfig projects with many files) this repeated linear scanning can accumulate.

Consider maintaining a `Set<string>` alongside `commandLine.fileNames` for O(1) membership checks, or converting `commandLine.fileNames` into a `Set` in a preprocessing step:
```typescript
const existing = new Set(this.commandLine.fileNames);
for (const fileName of fileNames) {
  if (!existing.has(fileName)) {
    this.commandLine.fileNames.push(fileName);
    existing.add(fileName);
    added = true;
  }
}
```

The module-level `componentMetaManager` singleton is only stored when `watch: true`. If a subsequent call comes in with `watch: true` after the previous manager was disposed (e.g., on Storybook config reload), the old `componentMetaManager` reference may be stale:

```typescript
async function createComponentMetaManager(watch: boolean) {
  if (componentMetaManager && watch) {
    return componentMetaManager; // returns stale disposed manager if dispose() was called externally
  }
  ...
}
```

Since `ComponentMetaManager.dispose()` sets internal state to disposed but doesn't nullify the module-level variable, calling `batchExtract` on a disposed manager would silently do nothing (projects map is cleared). Consider checking `componentMetaManager.isDisposed` (which doesn't currently exist) or nullifying `componentMetaManager` inside `dispose()` if this module owns its lifecycle.

`require.resolve('react/package.json')` is a CommonJS API. If this test helper is loaded as an ES module (which is the case when `package.json` declares `"type": "module"` or when Vitest transforms in ESM mode), `require` is not defined and this will throw a `ReferenceError` at module initialization time.

Use `createRequire` from `node:module` instead:
```typescript
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
const NODE_MODULES_DIR = path.resolve(require.resolve('react/package.json'), '../..');
```

In `extractStaticDefaultProps`, the loop over class members checks `member.name.getText() !== 'defaultProps'` but doesn't verify the `static` modifier. This means an instance property named `defaultProps` would also be collected, which is not a valid React pattern.

Add a modifier check:
```typescript
if (
  !typescript.isPropertyDeclaration(member) ||
  !member.name || member.name.getText() !== 'defaultProps' ||
  !typescript.getModifiers(member)?.some(m => m.kind === typescript.SyntaxKind.StaticKeyword)
) {
  continue;
}
```
